---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import MethodologyNotice from '../../components/MethodologyNotice.astro';
import '../../styles/content-pages.css';
import '../../styles/bench-methodology.css';
import { ARENAS, LEVEL_STATUSES, activeLevels, canonicalDenominator, operationalWeight } from '../../data/benchmarkSpec';
const arena=ARENAS.dataRetrieval, active=activeLevels(arena), denominator=canonicalDenominator(arena), activeWeight=operationalWeight(arena);
const attainable=(activeWeight/denominator*100).toFixed(2);
const formula=active.map(level=>`${level.weight===1?'':`${level.weight}×`}${level.name}`).join(' + ');
const protocols=arena.levels.map(level=>({...level,anchor:`level-${level.number}-${level.name.toLowerCase()}`}));
---
<Layout title="DATA Bench Methodology | Ultimate Bench" description="Administration, exact-answer adjudication, level protocols, scoring, security, interpretation, and limitations for DATA Bench.">
<main class="content-page bench-methodology data-methodology" data-data-methodology data-active-level-count={active.length} data-operational-weight={activeWeight} data-complete-weight={denominator}>
  <MethodologyNotice />
<header class="page-heading" id="overview"><span class="page-kicker">Arena 02 · Methodology</span><h1>DATA Bench Methodology</h1><p>How named chatbot products and interface configurations are evaluated on difficult, exact numerical information from obscure media-related sources.</p></header>
<nav class="section-navigation" aria-label="DATA Bench methodology sections">
<a href="#construct">What it measures</a><a href="#administration">Administration</a><a href="#adjudication">Exact answers</a><a href="#mapping">Answer mapping</a><a href="#confidentiality">Confidentiality</a><a href="#statuses">Statuses</a>
{protocols.map(level=><a href={`#${level.anchor}`} class={level.status===LEVEL_STATUSES.PLANNED?'pending':undefined}>Level {level.number} — {level.name} ({level.status})</a>)}
<a href="#weighting">Weighting</a><a href="#coverage">Coverage &amp; rank</a><a href="#limitations">Limitations</a><a href="#auditability">Auditability</a>
</nav>

<article class="content-card shared-methodology">
<section id="construct" aria-labelledby="construct-title"><p class="section-label">Scope</p><h2 id="construct-title">What DATA Bench evaluates</h2>
<p>DATA Bench evaluates the ability of a named chatbot product or interface configuration to obtain and report difficult exact numerical information from obscure media-related sources. Source domains include anime, manga, books, comics, and media more generally.</p>
<p>Across current and documented proposed tasks, success can require obscure textual retrieval, static visual inspection, online source discovery, understanding animation or temporally unfolding events, understanding and counting sounds, distinguishing versions or adaptations, resisting misleading numerical suggestions, managing several questions, binding each answer correctly, and combining retrieved values through arithmetic.</p>
<div class="callout"><strong>The evaluated object is the accessible product configuration.</strong><p>Results describe the named product, model/configuration, and interface—not an abstract foundation model evaluated through identical APIs.</p></div>
<h3>What it is not</h3><p>DATA Bench is not a comprehensive factuality benchmark, universal measure of research ability, pure web-search benchmark, pure visual benchmark, pure audiovisual-understanding benchmark, psychometrically validated test, guarantee on all obscure questions, or comparison of foundation models under identical APIs. Its compound levels do not isolate pure faculties.</p></section>

<section id="administration" aria-labelledby="administration-title"><p class="section-label">Shared procedure</p><h2 id="administration-title">Administration and interface policy</h2>
            <p><strong>Independent Active-level administration:</strong> every model/configuration is administered on every Active level regardless of earlier results. A score of 0% or an INVALID result does not stop testing and does not prevent administration of later Active levels. Planned and Locked levels receive no model results.</p>
<div class="procedure-grid"><div><strong>Fresh conversations</strong><span>every repetition is isolated</span></div><div><strong>Available tools permitted</strong><span>search and code are part of the interface</span></div><div><strong>Named configuration</strong><span>product conditions are recorded</span></div></div>
<ol><li>Every repetition begins in a fresh, isolated conversation. Previous runs, answers, scores, and conversation history are never supplied.</li><li>The exact named product, model/configuration, and interface configuration are recorded. The date is recorded where the results system supports it.</li><li>Web search is permitted whenever available in the tested interface. Code execution is permitted whenever the configuration provides it.</li><li>Google AI Studio configurations with and without code execution are separate named configurations. AI Studio temperature is set to <strong>1</strong>, consistently with Visual Bench.</li><li>Level-specific grouping, repetitions, and scoring follow the protocols below.</li></ol>
<h3>Provider-controlled conditions</h3><p>Consumer interfaces may independently control search, tool invocation, sampling, reasoning allocation, internal routing, context handling, multimedia processing, timeouts, hidden system instructions, caching, safety behavior, and model snapshot selection. These remain part of the tested environment. DATA Bench does not claim identical APIs, tool or reasoning budgets, latency, source access, or hidden processing.</p></section>

<section id="adjudication" aria-labelledby="adjudication-title"><p class="section-label">Binary adjudication</p><h2 id="adjudication-title">Exact, resolved numerical answers</h2>
<p>Every scored answer resolves to an exact numerical value—or, in the documented Level 5 design, an exact numerical product. Credit requires one definite numerical conclusion matching the predetermined answer. An explanation is acceptable when its conclusion is exact and unambiguous; number-only formatting is not required.</p>
<dl class="policy-list"><div><dt>Approximation or uncertainty</dt><dd>“Around 10,” “approximately 10,” “I think it is 10,” “either 9 or 10,” “probably 10,” and “10, but I am not sure” receive no credit. “9 or 10, probably 10” fails regardless of the authoritative value because it remains unresolved. This is an exact-answer requirement, not a penalty for conversational style.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Accurate qualifications</dt><dd>“10, excluding [a specified character]” may be accepted if factually correct and consistent with the item. A qualification that changes the question, is false, or creates ambiguity receives no credit.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Equivalent representations</dt><dd>Digits and number words, integers and equivalent decimals, equivalent fractions or percentages, and correctly converted units are accepted where appropriate. For example, 10 metres and 1,000 centimetres are equivalent. A bare number uses the requested unit.</dd></div>
<div><dt>Conflicting candidates</dt><dd>Multiple incompatible values receive no credit unless one is clearly selected. The evaluator does not choose whichever candidate matches the key.</dd></div></dl></section>

<section id="mapping" aria-labelledby="mapping-title"><p class="section-label">Multi-question prompts</p><h2 id="mapping-title">Answer-to-question mapping</h2>
<p>Each answer must be objectively attributable to its intended question or section from the response itself. Numbering, labels, tables, repeated question language, explicit references, and rigorous original order are acceptable. Numbering is not mandatory when another method is clear.</p><p>If answers are simultaneously out of order, unnumbered, unlabeled, and unsupported by identifying language, the evaluator does not reconstruct a plausible mapping. Only objectively attributable answers receive credit.</p></section>

<section id="confidentiality" aria-labelledby="confidentiality-title"><p class="section-label">Benchmark security</p><h2 id="confidentiality-title">Transparent design, protected active material</h2>
<p>This page discloses the construct, administration counts, isolation, tool policy, broad source categories, exact-answer and mapping rules, misdirection design, documented arithmetic structure, scoring, weighting, statuses, limitations, and audit expectations.</p><p>Active questions, exact answers, fixed misleading suggestions, private source references, scene locations, exact counting cases, item pairings, answer keys, run-level outputs, and details enabling direct memorization or targeted optimization remain confidential. The methodology explains the test without becoming an answer key.</p></section>

<section id="statuses" aria-labelledby="statuses-title"><p class="section-label">Canonical result states</p><h2 id="statuses-title">Statuses are not interchangeable</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Numeric 0–100</th><td>Completed valid result; zero is observed.</td><td>Weighted and provides Active coverage.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">INVALID</th><td>Attempted evaluation stored as invalid.</td><td>Zero points; weight and coverage retained.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">UNAVAILABLE</th><td>Configuration inaccessible.</td><td>Zero; denominator remains 63; incomplete coverage.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">NOT_TESTED</th><td>Active level not administered.</td><td>Zero; denominator remains 63; incomplete coverage.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Missing</th><td>Required Active field absent.</td><td>Validation error, not a status.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Planned</th><td>Non-operational level without a model result.</td><td>Reserved capacity; no coverage effect.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The public repository establishes no DATA-specific INVALID trigger, so none is invented here. Planned levels are never represented as model-level zero, INVALID, UNAVAILABLE, or NOT_TESTED.</p></section>
</article>

<section class="protocols" aria-label="DATA Bench level protocols">
<article id="level-1-worm" class="content-card protocol" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="worm-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 1 · weight 1</p><h2 id="worm-title">Worm</h2><p>Individually administered exact retrieval.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and task</h3><p>Ten distinct obscure-trivia questions from anime, manga, books, comics, and media generally have predetermined exact numerical answers. They may depend on obscure textual or static visual details.</p></section><section><h3>Prompt and administration</h3><p>Each question is asked individually ten times and is never grouped with the others. Every administration starts fresh: 10 questions × 10 repetitions = <strong>100 administrations</strong> and 100 scored responses.</p></section><section><h3>Scoring and adjudication</h3><p>Each response earns one point or zero under shared exactness and certainty rules. Available tools follow shared policy. Maximum: 100.</p></section><section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>Worm measures repeatable exact retrieval while minimizing multi-question interference. Unlike Visual Bench’s grouped ten-question administration, each Worm conversation contains one question. The fixed set does not represent all obscure information.</p></section></article>

<article id="level-2-koala" class="content-card protocol" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="koala-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 2 · weight 2</p><h2 id="koala-title">Koala</h2><p>The Worm question set, administered together.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and task</h3><p>Koala uses the exact same ten questions as Worm, fixing the items while changing response-management burden.</p></section><section><h3>Prompt and administration</h3><p>All ten questions appear together in one prompt. The complete prompt is administered ten times in fresh conversations: <strong>10 conversations</strong> and 100 scored answers.</p></section><section><h3>Scoring and adjudication</h3><p>Each answer is independent; maximum 100. Worm’s exactness, certainty, units, and equivalence rules remain. Each answer must map objectively; numbering is optional when exact order or another method is clear.</p></section><section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>Koala adds multi-question handling, answer binding, order preservation, and resistance to cross-question interference. It does not isolate pure memory or attention.</p></section></article>

<article id="level-3-crow" class="content-card protocol" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="crow-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 3 · weight 4</p><h2 id="crow-title">Crow</h2><p>Grouped retrieval under fixed false numerical anchoring.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and task</h3><p>Crow uses the same ten questions as Worm and Koala, but every question includes a deliberately incorrect suggested number. The same fixed suggestion is used for that question every time. It is not random; it was selected because models commonly mistake it for the answer.</p></section><section><h3>Prompt and administration</h3><p>All ten questions are together. The complete prompt runs ten times in fresh conversations: 100 scored answers, maximum 100.</p></section><section><h3>Scoring and adjudication</h3><p>No explicit refutation is required. A correct exact certain number suffices; repeating or accepting the suggestion earns no credit. Mapping rules apply.</p></section><section><h3>Interpretation and limitations</h3><p>Crow adds resistance to misleading user anchoring, sycophantic agreement, uncritical validation, and premature acceptance of a plausible false answer. It still depends on Koala abilities and does not isolate anchoring resistance.</p></section></article>

<article id="level-4-octopus" class="content-card protocol" data-level-status="Active" aria-labelledby="octopus-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">Active · Level 4 · weight 8</p><h2 id="octopus-title">Octopus</h2><p>Harder new retrieval and media investigation under fixed misdirection.</p></header>
<section><h3>Purpose and task</h3><p>Octopus uses ten entirely new, intentionally harder questions. Some concern difficult text or static visuals; some require sound, animation or temporally unfolding events, or counting repeated sounds, actions, movements, or other scene events. Not every item is audiovisual.</p></section><section><h3>Prompt and administration</h3><p>All ten appear together, each with a fixed misleading number following Crow’s rule. The full prompt runs ten times fresh: 100 scored answers, maximum 100.</p></section><section><h3>Source and version control</h3><p>Media is not supplied. The product must locate the source or scene online using its available tools. Exact answer, source version, and counting convention are established beforehand. Version may be explicit—anime versus manga, language, adaptation, or edition—or clear from context. Evaluators never revise the authoritative version or convention after a response.</p></section><section><h3>Scoring, interpretation, and limitations</h3><p>Shared exact and mapping rules apply. Octopus compounds source discovery, obscure retrieval, static inspection, temporal audiovisual understanding, counting, version control, misdirection resistance, and answer binding. New text/static questions are not lower-level equivalents.</p></section></article>

<article id="level-5-raven" class="content-card protocol" data-level-status="Planned" aria-labelledby="raven-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">Planned · Level 5 · reserved weight 16</p><h2 id="raven-title">Raven</h2><p>Documented proposed multiplication design; not an Active protocol or scored level.</p></header>
<section><h3>Canonical status</h3><p>Raven is <strong>Planned</strong> canonically. It has no finalized operational protocol, model results, operational weight, coverage effect, or rank effect. The documented proposed design below is preserved for a future version; it is not evidence of administration or activation.</p></section><section><h3>Proposed prompt structure</h3><p>The single prompt begins with an instruction equivalent to: “I am going to give you 10 sections of text, each containing two questions. For each section, figure out the numerical answer to both questions, multiply those two numbers together, and output the final product. Please output the 10 final numbers in order.” The instruction and all sections are one prompt, not separate messages.</p><p>Ten sections contain two numerical questions each: 20 retrieval questions per administration. Each section requires both values, their product, and order preservation. Intended items include some hardest questions from Levels 1–3, some hardest from Level 4, and some new questions. Unlike Levels 3–4, there are no misleading suggestions.</p></section><section><h3>Proposed administration and scoring</h3><p>The complete prompt would run ten times in fresh conversations. Ten final products per run yield 100 scored products, maximum 100. Only the product scores: one correct component, or two correct components with wrong multiplication, earns no partial credit.</p><p>Work and explanations are permitted but unnecessary. Products must be exact and mapped by numbers, labels, tables, or exact order; unordered unlabeled output is not inferred.</p></section><section><h3>Intended interpretation and limitation</h3><p>The design compounds difficult textual and audiovisual retrieval, pairwise binding, arithmetic, ten-section organization, and order compliance. It would not measure arithmetic independently. Activation requires a deliberate benchmark-version change and results migration; none occurs here.</p></section></article>

<article id="level-6-athena" class="content-card protocol" data-level-status="Planned" aria-labelledby="athena-title"><header><p class="protocol-level">Planned · Level 6 · reserved weight 32</p><h2 id="athena-title">Athena</h2><p>No finalized task or operational protocol.</p></header><section><h3>Status</h3><p>Athena is Planned and not properly designed. It has no finalized active task, active prompt, operational administration protocol, finalized scoring rules, or model result. It does not affect coverage or rank eligibility; weight 32 is reserved within denominator 63.</p></section><section><h3>Activation boundary</h3><p>No Athena score should appear, and no future capability, task, prompt, or activation rule should be inferred. A complete protocol must be designed, documented, reviewed, and introduced by deliberate benchmark-version change before activation.</p></section></article>
</section>

<article class="content-card closing-methodology">
<section id="weighting" aria-labelledby="weighting-title"><p class="section-label">Fixed six-level normalization</p><h2 id="weighting-title">Progressive Level Weighting</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Level</th><th scope="col">Weight</th><th scope="col">Canonical status</th></tr></thead><tbody>{arena.levels.map(level=><tr><th scope="row">{level.number} — {level.name}</th><td>{level.weight}</td><td>{level.status}</td></tr>)}<tr class="total"><th scope="row">Permanent denominator</th><td>{denominator}</td><td>All six positions</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><code>DATA score = ({formula}) / {denominator}</code></p><p>The formula is derived from Active levels. DATA currently has operational weight <strong>{activeWeight}/{denominator}</strong>, so perfect Active performance earns at most <strong>{attainable}%</strong>. Raven weight 16 and Athena weight 32 are reserved without model-level zeroes.</p><p>Powers of two are editorial importance weights, not empirical psychometric difficulty estimates. They do not mean each level is literally twice as difficult.</p></section>
<section id="coverage" aria-labelledby="coverage-title"><p class="section-label">Qualification</p><h2 id="coverage-title">Coverage, provisional results, and ranking</h2><p>Numeric zero and INVALID retain weight and coverage. UNAVAILABLE and NOT_TESTED contribute zero without reducing denominator 63 but leave coverage incomplete. Such results remain visible as provisional and receive no official rank.</p><p>Official DATA rank requires every Active DATA level. Overall qualification requires rank eligibility in all three arenas. Equal unrounded scores share rank; tie presentation order creates no different rank. Full precision is retained until display rounding. Planned Raven and Athena are excluded from Active coverage.</p></section>
<section id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-title"><p class="section-label">Boundaries</p><h2 id="limitations-title">Interpretation and limitations</h2><ul><li>The set is fixed and confidential. Repetition measures those items, not independently sampled populations.</li><li>Browsing, media access, code, and indexing may confer advantages; tools are part of the product.</li><li>Provider updates change results; sources can disappear, change, be geo-blocked, or differ by version.</li><li>Audio, subtitle, dub, adaptation, edition, and scene versions matter. Predetermined versions reduce ambiguity but are not universally representative.</li><li>Manual adjudication may be needed. Exact scoring intentionally rejects approximate knowledge.</li><li>Crow and Octopus test one kind of false anchoring, not all sycophancy.</li><li>Proposed Raven combines retrieval and arithmetic, so causes of errors cannot always be separated.</li><li>Small differences should not be overinterpreted; the benchmark does not prove universal superiority.</li></ul></section>
<section id="auditability" aria-labelledby="audit-title"><p class="section-label">Internal records</p><h2 id="audit-title">Auditability without public leakage</h2><p>Where available, internal records should preserve benchmark version, level, private item or prompt-set ID, product and model/configuration, interface, date, search and code availability, configurable temperature, run number, per-item or per-section score, final total, status, adjudication notes, source-version notes, and necessary counting-convention notes.</p><p>This prospective standard does not claim every historical field exists. Questions, keys, source scenes, misleading values, and confidential outputs must remain outside public source, client bundles, and static output. No public DATA record schema exists here; this page fabricates no historical records or competing private system.</p></section>
</article>
</main></Layout>
